 You can pass as many column-name column-type pairs as you like. Valid types are ``integer``, ``text``, ``float`` and ``blob``.
 
+Use ``--strict`` to create a `STRICT table <https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html>`__.
+STRICT tables reject values that cannot be losslessly converted to the declared
+column type. The ``float`` type is created as ``REAL`` because ``FLOAT`` is not
+one of the column types supported by STRICT tables::
+
+    $ sqlite-utils create-table mydb.db measurements \
+        id integer \
+        value float \
+        --pk=id \
+        --strict
+
+The ``sqlite-utils insert`` and ``sqlite-utils upsert`` commands also accept
+``--strict``. It takes effect when the command creates a new table.
+
 You can specify columns that should be NOT NULL using ``--not-null colname``. You can specify default values for columns using ``--default colname defaultvalue``.
 
