+The configuration options that can be specified in this way are ``pk``, ``foreign_keys``, ``column_order``, ``not_null``, ``defaults``, ``batch_size``, ``hash_id``, ``alter``, ``ignore``, ``replace``, ``extracts``, ``conversions``, ``columns``, ``strict``. These are all documented below.
+
+Creating STRICT tables
+----------------------
+
+SQLite 3.37 and higher supports `STRICT tables <https://www.sqlite.org/stricttables.html>`__,
+which reject values that cannot be losslessly converted to the declared column
+type. Pass ``strict=True`` to any method that can create a table::
+
+    db["measurements"].insert(
+        {"id": 1, "value": 3.14},
+        pk="id",
+        strict=True,
+    )
+
+The option is supported by ``db.create_table()``, ``table.create()``,
+``table.insert()``, ``table.insert_all()``, ``table.upsert()`` and
+``table.upsert_all()``. It can also be configured as a table default using
+``db.table("measurements", strict=True)``. Python ``float`` columns use the
+STRICT-compatible SQLite ``REAL`` type.
 
